0 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120929 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120929 #edb/ref 1061 486583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120929 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b0251d11-c37d-5bc9-839f-3b319b92b170" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120929 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120930 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120930 #edb/ref 1061 486584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120930 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f33e53a7-67fa-52bd-883d-157e2b1bfbc6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120930 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120931 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120931 #edb/ref 1061 486587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120931 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "34f75092-da67-553a-bcea-fdad4bc5ae09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120931 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120932 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120932 #edb/ref 1061 486590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120932 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3987c34c-0de9-5447-a8ac-de5808efdd56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120932 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120933 #edb/ref 1061 486593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120933 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c1ff9805-1591-512e-a111-06479708d986" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120933 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120973 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120973 #edb/ref 1061 826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120973 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0eafffd9-cdc6-547a-8dec-831c2199ceb7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120973 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120974 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120974 #edb/ref 1061 827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120974 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9d15864d-ad01-5b37-b389-64a195db4514" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120974 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120975 #edb/ref 1061 831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120975 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c53198f3-9bc3-55ad-9773-4cbd012d550a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120975 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121014 #edb/ref 1064 "To determine the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we first cancel any common factors in the numerator and denominator. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x$.\n\nFor example, let's try to find the vertical asymptotes of the rational function $y=\\dfrac{2}{x-5}.$ \n\nNow, there are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, and so we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =x-5 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is a vertical asymptote at $x=5.$\n\nThis matches up  with what we see in the graph of the function:\n\n![](images/e9/e9d2c264e323eb86b4e56fef563387989f66fb016a3e1c98db1f35776e3b5cb5.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121014 #edb/ref 1065 264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121014 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "22321890-d586-5e6c-bff9-b2a6b3388ae0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121014 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121072 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we have a function $f(x) = x^2+2x,$ and we want to find the derivative of the *inverse* function $(f^{-1})'(x).$ Can we do this without explicitly computing the inverse?\n\nIndeed, we can! The trick is to write the inverse function *implicitly*, differentiate it, and then write the result in terms of $f^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y = x^2+2x,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=y^2+2y\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y=f^{-1}(x).$ Also, remember that swapping $x$ and $y$ gives us the inverse function because the inverse function $f^{-1}(x)$ can be obtained by reflecting the graph of $f(x)$ over the line $y=x.$\n\n![](images/56/56c3c733ec9ed00563b5f5c786175898ac6e564658d1a70d4105f749664436ab.png)\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we start by computing $\\dfrac{ \\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={y}^{2}+2y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}+2y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =2y+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ we take the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}y}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}y} = 2y + 2 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{2y+2}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of $f^{-1}(x).$ Since $y=f^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2y+2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{f}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{2{f}^{-1}(x)+2} \\\\ ({f}^{-1})^{'}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2{f}^{-1}(x)+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121072 #edb/ref 1065 443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121072 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "28adec60-b1b0-5bee-949f-2d6ac5eefe88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121072 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121132 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that a curve is defined by the following equation:\n\n$$\nx= y^3+y\n$$\n\nWe say that the equation of this curve is defined **implicitly** because instead of the usual [math]y = y(x),[/math] we have [math]x = x(y).[/math] Sometimes, it's convenient to define a curve this way. You'll meet more implicit curves later.\n\nTo graph this curve, we can start by selecting some values of [math]y[/math] and computing the corresponding [math]x[/math]-values as follows:\n\n\\begin{align}\n\ty=0 & \\quad \\longrightarrow\\quad  x=0^3+0=0 \\\\[5pt]\n\ty=1 & \\quad\\longrightarrow\\quad x=1^3+1=2  \\\\[5pt]\n\ty=2 & \\quad\\longrightarrow\\quad x=2^3+2=10 \\\\[5pt]\n\\end{align}\n\nSo we get the points [math](0,0),[/math] [math](2,1),[/math] and [math](10,2).[/math] If we plot these points and connect them with a smooth curve, we get the following graph:\n\nPlotting this curve in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/81/812db123dd422ff781472c73156a2f4d6e88a99aa3726668cd7a8b8882d6e0a8.png)\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent to this curve at a point, we need to follow two steps.\n\n- First, we calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy}$ by differentiating the equation of the curve with respect to $y.$\n\n- Then, we find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx}$ by taking the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac1{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy}\\right)}\n$$\n\nWe'll explore some intuition behind this result at the end of the lesson, but for now, let's focus on carrying out the computation.\n\nWe start by differentiating $x=y^3+y$ with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y) \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take its reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3{y}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative is also defined implicitly. In other words, $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx}$ is a function of $y,$ not $x.$\n\nTo calculate the slope of the tangent to this curve at a point, say $(2,{\\color{blue}{1}}),$ we substitute ${\\color{blue}{y}} = {\\color{blue}{1}}$ into our expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(2,1)} & =\\frac{1}{3(1)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(2,1)$ equals $\\dfrac14.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121132 #edb/ref 1065 7757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121132 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5b0053f2-47f3-5689-bf17-ed5cb69d36ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121132 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121133 #edb/ref 1064 "Throughout this lesson, we've made use of the following result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy} \\right)^{-1}\n$$\n\nLet's now build some more intuition behind this result.\n\nConsider the graph of $x=f(y),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f1/f11b52c2b788fcf1a9a161fa46843d42c10973545f2ff923750bcb4c50b9faca.png)\n\nThe slope of the tangent to $x=f(y)$ at $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{rise}}{\\text{run}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d y}\n$$\n\n \n\nrepresents the change in $x$ *per unit change* in $y$ at $P.$ According to our diagram, this is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{run}}{\\text{rise}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the slope of the tangent to the curve at $P$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{rise}}{\\text{run}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\text{run}}{\\text{rise}})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result can also be proved formally using the so-called **chain rule**." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121133 #edb/ref 1065 7758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121133 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2683a80b-0267-5fa3-b804-8d7b2373d85b" 